
--> Spring Boot Caching
        What is Caching?
        Caching means storing frequently used data in fast-access storage so that repeated requests don’t hit the database every time.

        In-Memory Cache vs Distributed Cache
        | Feature      | In-Memory Cache         | Distributed Cache |
        | ------------ | ----------------------- | ----------------- |
        | Storage      | Same JVM memory         | External server   |
        | Speed        | Very fast               | Slightly slower   |
        | Scalability  | ❌ Limited               | ✅ High            |
        | Data sharing | ❌ No                    | ✅ Yes             |
        | Example      | ConcurrentMap, Caffeine | **Redis**         |

    Read-through vs Write-through Cache
    Read-through
    App checks cache first
    If miss → DB → cache updated → return data
    Write-through
    Data written to cache and DB together
    Ensures consistency
    Spring Boot caching mostly behaves like read-through.

--> Cache Hit & Cache Miss
    Cache Hit → Data found in cache → FAST 🚀
    Cache Miss → Data not found → DB call → cache updated

--> TTL & Eviction Policies
    TTL (Time To Live)
    Time after which cache entry expires
    Example: 10 minutes
    Eviction Policies
    LRU (Least Recently Used)
    LFU (Least Frequently Used)
    TTL-based eviction

